++# Phase 3 — what the live fault rig found (orchestrator ran it; 1 of 5 passed)
++
++Live rig result (builds/agent-command/rig/run.mjs, real cmux + real CLIs):
++- long follow-up (codex) expected done, got no_start 61.5s · permission (claude) expected stuck, got timeout 15.3s · silent turn (claude) expected stalled, got timeout 45.4s · missing binary passed · question (codex) expected done, got no_start.
++Root cause, confirmed by opening Sol and Opus by hand in a brand-new git repo: both CLIs stop on a folder-trust dialog before any session or hook exists ("Trust this folder? 1. Trust and continue / 2. Quit" for Codex; "No, exit / Yes, I trust this folder" for Claude). `wait` cannot see it from transcripts or cmux events, so it reports no_start/timeout. Real worktrees of a trusted repo don't prompt (trust follows the repo); brand-new repos and untrusted folders do. Also observed earlier: Codex writes nothing to its rollout while it reasons, so a long think looked like `stalled` (false alarm).
++
++Do:
++1. Screen check for dialogs that come before the session: when a worker has no session yet (and in every `wait` poll until it does, at least every 3s), read its screen; if it shows a trust/permission dialog (Codex trust text, Claude trust text, Grok's equivalent if any — collect the exact strings from the CLIs or their bundled text; a test fixture per harness), return `stuck` with `kind: "trust"` and the dialog lines, within 10s of it appearing. Never answer it: trusting writes the CLI's config; that is the user's call.
++2. False stall: before returning `stalled` for a Codex worker, read its screen; if it shows Codex's running indicator ("Working (" with an elapsed time that advanced since the previous poll), it is thinking, not stalled: keep waiting. Same idea for Claude's and Grok's running indicators. `stalled` = running turn + no transcript growth + no advancing indicator for --stall-after.
++3. Rig: create its scratch folder as a `git worktree add --detach` of the current repo under $TMPDIR (trust follows the repo), not a fresh `git init`; remove the worktree at the end. Add a 6th scenario: a fresh `git init` folder → expect `stuck` kind trust within 15s (then close).
++4. The permission scenario: confirm why Claude reported `timeout` at 15s rather than `stuck` even in the earlier pane tests the PermissionRequest hook arrived ~4s in; check whether the hook needs the session to exist (trust dialog first) — after fix 3 it should pass; if not, find out and fix.
++Same constraints, tests and commit grouping as before. Don't run the rig; the orchestrator will. Reply DONE.
